      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="background">1. Background &mdash; what went wrong</h2>
      <p>
        An attempt to reduce duplicated JavaScript and make every AI surface share
        one code path broke the chat system. Two defects, both now fixed:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Lowercase <code>Process</code> in Template Toolkit.</strong> All three
            call sites used <code>[&#37; Process('ai/model_select.tt', ... ) &#37;]</code>. TT
            directives are UPPERCASE; lowercase parses as an undefined variable and
            renders nothing, so every model dropdown was empty.</li>
        <li><strong>The detached popup bypassed the loader.</strong>
            <code>/ai/widget</code> renders <code>widget.tt</code> through a bare
            <code>Template-&gt;new</code> with no Catalyst context, so it never saw the
            catalog stash or the cache-buster and served a frozen
            <code>?v=1</code> with an empty widget.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-warning">
        Lesson recorded: static analysis and <code>node --check</code> cannot catch
        either failure. Only rendering the live page proves a fix.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="current">2. Current state (delivered)</h2>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><code>Comserv::Util::ModelCatalog</code> is the single source of truth for the
            model list. Built once per process from the v2 Router, cached with a
            600-second TTL, primed for free by <code>/ai2/providers</code>.</li>
        <li><code>root/ai/model_select.tt</code> is the single server-rendered dropdown
            partial used by the floating widget, the <code>/ai/widget</code> popup,
            <code>/ai</code> and <code>/ai2</code>.</li>
        <li>Non-chat model families (embeddings, rerankers, image/audio/video,
            moderation) are filtered out: 414 catalog entries &rarr; 397 options.</li>
        <li>Each entry carries cost flags: <code>free</code>, <code>local</code>,
            <code>paid</code>. Free models sort first inside their provider group and
            are labelled &ldquo;&mdash; free&rdquo;; Ollama models are labelled
            &ldquo;&mdash; local&rdquo;.</li>
        <li><code>default_for()</code> pre-selects a free OpenRouter model for guests and
            ordinary members, and the pinned coding model for privileged users on a
            coding surface.</li>
        <li><strong>Phase 1 delivered (AIMPS-P1 / #253).</strong> OpenRouter's
            <code>pricing</code> hash from <code>/api/v1/models</code> is no longer
            discarded: <code>OpenRouter::list_models</code> keeps the raw
            <code>pricing</code> object and adds <code>price_prompt</code> /
            <code>price_completion</code> (USD per 1M tokens). The v2 Router carries both
            through <code>get_available_models</code>, and <code>ModelCatalog::_flatten</code>
            exposes them on every catalog entry alongside a threshold-derived
            <code>price_tier</code> (<code>free</code> / <code>cheap</code> &le; $1 / 1M,
            <code>mid</code> &le; $5 / 1M, <code>premium</code> &gt; $5 / 1M — ranked by the
            dearer of prompt/completion). Tiers are threshold-driven, never a hardcoded
            model list. See changelog
            <a href="[% c.uri_for('/Documentation/changelog/2026-08-10-openrouter-pricing-feed') %]">2026-08-10 OpenRouter pricing feed</a>.
            A same-day follow-up fixed <code>Controller::AI2::providers</code>, which was
            stripping <code>price_*</code> / <code>pricing</code> out of the catalog before
            priming <code>ModelCatalog</code> &mdash; that is why the dropdown initially
            showed the provider but a blank fee (not a cache issue).</li>
        <li><strong>Phase 2 price display delivered (AIMPS-P2 / #254).</strong> Paid
            models now show their real per-token cost. The widget, <code>/ai</code>,
            <code>/ai2</code> and the git dashboard render their dropdown via the shared
            <code>ai-chat/model-select.js</code> module (it rebuilds the
            <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> from <code>window.ComservConfig.models</code> on
            <code>init()</code>, so the server-rendered <code>ai/model_select.tt</code> is
            overwritten on those surfaces). That module now carries
            <code>price_prompt</code>/<code>price_completion</code>/<code>pricing</code>
            through <code>fromFlat()</code> and appends
            <code>— $pp/$pc per 1M (tier)</code> (with <code>— free</code> /
            <code>— local</code>) in <code>render()</code>; the provider is shown once via the
            optgroup header (the doubled-provider bug is fixed). <code>/ai</code>,
            <code>/ai2</code> and <code>/admin/git</code> now emit the
            <code>ComservConfig.models</code> global so the fast path is used. The
            server-rendered <code>ai/model_select.tt</code> also shows the price for any
            surface that uses it directly. Remaining P2 work (premium-tier &ldquo;show
            all&rdquo; toggle, tier-then-capability ordering) is still open.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="doc-h3">Why free OpenRouter beats Ollama for guests</h3>
      <p>
        Ollama costs no money but it is <em>not</em> free: it consumes workstation
        (192.168.1.199) GPU and VRAM, and that host is routinely saturated. Sending
        anonymous guest traffic there degrades the machine we depend on for real
        work while giving the guest the slowest possible answer. A free OpenRouter
        model costs nothing, runs off-host, and responds faster. Local models stay
        available in the list for anyone who explicitly wants privacy or offline
        operation &mdash; they are simply no longer the silent default.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="p1">3. Phase 1 &mdash; Cost metadata &amp; pricing feed</h2>
      <p>
        OpenRouter's <code>/api/v1/models</code> response already includes a
        <code>pricing</code> object (per-token prompt and completion cost); our
        provider currently discards it. Phase 1 keeps it and surfaces it on every
        catalog entry so later phases can sort, filter and display by real cost.
      </p>
      <table class="doc-table">
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        <tr><td>price_prompt</td><td>USD per 1M input tokens</td></tr>
        <tr><td>price_completion</td><td>USD per 1M output tokens</td></tr>
        <tr><td>price_tier</td><td>free / cheap / mid / premium, derived from thresholds</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="doc-note">
        Tiers must be threshold-driven, never a hardcoded list of model names &mdash;
        the OpenRouter catalog changes weekly and any name list will rot.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="p2">4. Phase 2 &mdash; Cost-aware sorting, filtering &amp; price display</h2>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Show the per-1M cost beside each paid model so the user sees the price
            before choosing.</li>
        <li>Hide the premium tier behind a &ldquo;show all models&rdquo; toggle. 387
            OpenRouter entries in one dropdown is not a usable interface.</li>
        <li>Replace alphabetical ordering with tier-then-capability ordering. The
            current alphabetical sort is why the list opens on &ldquo;AI21:&nbsp;&hellip;&rdquo;.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="p3">5. Phase 3 &mdash; Page and role scoped shortlists</h2>
      <p>
        The model shown should depend on what the user is actually doing. Editing a
        Perl module and writing a herb monograph do not need the same shortlist.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Role visibility delivered (AIMPS-P3 / #255).</strong> The shared catalog
            (<code>Comserv::Util::ModelCatalog</code>) now role-filters at the single source of
            truth, so a not-logged-in user no longer sees the full admin list. Tiers:
            <strong>guest</strong> &rarr; free OpenRouter + Ollama local only (no paid, no Grok);
            <strong>member</strong> (logged-in, non-privileged) &rarr; free + cheap + mid
            (premium &gt; $5 / 1M excluded, no Grok); <strong>privileged</strong>
            (admin/developer/editor) &rarr; everything. Verified live: an unauthenticated
            request to the widget returns 19 models (5 Ollama + 14 free OpenRouter), 0 paid,
            0 Grok &mdash; down from the full 414-model admin list. See changelog
            <a href="[% c.uri_for('/Documentation/changelog/2026-08-10-model-role-filter') %]">2026-08-10 model role filter</a>.
            Page-scoped shortlists (editor &rarr; coding models, herb/content &rarr; long-context)
            are a follow-up still open.</li>
      </ul>
      <table class="doc-table">
        <tr><th>Surface</th><th>Model class</th><th>Default</th></tr>
        <tr><td>AI2 code editor</td><td>Coding models</td><td>openrouter|tencent/hy3 (privileged only)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Herb / content pages</td><td>General + long-context</td><td>Free OpenRouter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Planning / todo</td><td>General reasoning</td><td>Free OpenRouter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Git dashboard</td><td>Coding + summarisation</td><td>Per role</td></tr>
        <tr><td>General chat (guest)</td><td>Free tier only</td><td>Free OpenRouter</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="doc-warning">
        All of this logic lives in <code>Model::AI2::Router</code>. No per-page bespoke
        AI logic &mdash; that fragmentation is exactly what caused the outage this plan
        follows.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-note">
        Related defect (todo&nbsp;#2036): the chat backend currently reports the generic
        bucket name <code>external</code> instead of the concrete provider that served
        the request, so the UI cannot distinguish OpenRouter from a future provider.
        This is worked around client-side today and must be fixed in the Router.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="p4">6. Phase 4 &mdash; Usage telemetry &amp; popularity ranking</h2>
      <p>
        Record the model that actually served each request &mdash; provider, surface,
        role, latency, success or failure &mdash; then rank the dropdown by observed
        usage and reliability rather than by guesswork. Includes a recurring monthly
        re-audit of the catalog, pricing tiers and defaults.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="p5">7. Phase 5 &mdash; Standing rules on every code touch</h2>
      <p>These are not one-off tasks. They apply to every future change.</p>

      <h3 class="doc-h3">Rule V2 &mdash; migrate as you touch</h3>
      <p>
        Any file touched on an AI surface must be moved onto the v2
        Router / ModelCatalog pattern as part of that change. No new v1 code paths,
        no new per-page model fetches, no new bespoke dispatch. Partial migration is
        how the system ended up with two disagreeing catalog shapes.
      </p>

      <h3 class="doc-h3">Rule DOC &mdash; docs match code, in the same commit</h3>
      <p>
        Every code change updates the matching <code>/Documentation/*.tt</code> page and
        appends to <code>/Documentation/CHANGELOG</code> in the <em>same</em> commit. A
        change whose documentation still describes the previous behaviour is not
        finished. Plans and docs must always describe the code as it currently is,
        not as it was intended to be.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-note">
        Enforcement: a pre-merge check flags any commit that modifies
        <code>lib/</code> or <code>root/static/js</code> under an AI path without a
        corresponding <code>.tt</code> documentation change.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2" id="questions">8. Open questions</h2>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Do members with their own API key bypass the premium filter entirely?</li>
        <li>Should price display be absolute dollars or a relative indicator
            (&#36;/&#36;&#36;/&#36;&#36;&#36;) for non-technical users?</li>
        <li>Per-site or per-role monthly spend cap, and what happens on breach?</li>
        <li>Should the free-tier preference order be configurable rather than a
            constant in <code>ModelCatalog</code>?</li>
      </ul>

